{"author":{"id":"f943aa1c5ee078b881a8e97535a4e2ccb401d01ca00f4128126befa9cca58a32"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792577950,"nonce":"9bvc2WGHCXcw1KUI/1e/MN6zRro=","metadata":{"github-url":"https://github.com/guilbep/git-board/issues/37","origin":"github","sync:github.com/guilbep/git-board:id":"I_kwDOUJip488AAAABYynGlg","sync:github.com/guilbep/git-board:labels":"","sync:github.com/guilbep/git-board:number":"37","sync:github.com/guilbep/git-board:status":"open","sync:github.com/guilbep/git-board:text":"86e793f7487ecc1a342dcf8303405be41d9f9ce2cca45fd17b35793fbed025a7","sync:github.com/guilbep/git-board:title":"release: in a fork's clone, releases go to its upstream origin","sync:github.com/guilbep/git-board:url":"https://github.com/guilbep/git-board/issues/37"},"title":"release: in a fork's clone, releases go to its upstream origin","message":"Seen 2026-10-21 in the clone of the git-bug fork, `~/works/git/github/git-bug`, while trying to cut `v0.11.1-sync.9`. That clone has two remotes: `origin`, which is upstream `git@github.com:git-bug/git-bug.git`, and `fork`, which is ours, `git@github.com:guilbep/git-bug.git`. The release was finally made by hand: tag pushed to `fork`, then the fork's release workflow.\n\n## What the Releases page shows\n- The header reads \". · published on GitHub\", with no repository name.\n- \"All releases\" lists only upstream's tags, `v0.7.2` to `v0.11.0`, all \"before git-board\". The fork's own tags, `v0.11.1-sync.6` to `.8`, which are pre-release tags on `fork`, are missing.\n- \"Cut a release\" offers `v0.11.1`, not `v0.11.1-sync.9`.\n- The cut stopped with git-bug's CLI text: \"No identity is set. To interact with bugs, an identity first needs to be created using \"git bug user new\" or adopted with \"git bug user adopt\"\". git-board has its own identity picker in Settings, but the message doesn't point to it.\n- On the Sync page, the Remotes panel says \"Not done. The git-board server is not reachable.\" for this repository. Its Check on `fork` never ran (\"Not checked yet\").\n\n## Why, from the code\n- The release flow's authority remote is `origin` whenever a remote of that name exists (`internal/release/binaries.go:68`). In a fork's clone, `origin` is upstream, so a cut reads upstream's tags and would push its tag and publish its release on `git-bug/git-bug`.\n- The `[release] remote` key in `.git-board.toml` can name another remote (`Cfg.RemoteName()`). Nothing suggests it on a fork, and `ConfigRepo` still hardcodes `origin` (`internal/release/git.go:176, 179, 250`).\n\n## Expected\n- **The authority remote is chosen, not assumed.** When `origin` isn't the user's (a fork's upstream they can't push to) and another remote is, Settings suggests `[release] remote = \"fork\"` and says why. A cut never pushes a tag or publishes on a remote the token can't write: it stops and says so.\n- **Pre-release tags count.** Tags such as `-sync.N` are listed and the next one is proposed (`v0.11.1-sync.9`), or the scheme can be set.\n- **A missing identity sends the user to Settings › identity**, not to a CLI command.\n- **The header names the repository.**\n\nWorkaround, not tried yet: `[release] remote = \"fork\"` in the fork clone's `.git-board.toml`. Test only on scratch repositories, never by cutting a release on `git-bug/git-bug`.","files":null}]}